      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You made an excellent point that I should not have allowed to slip into the response in the previous turn (the \"high-fidelity\" and \"f- and f-\" claims were hallucinated). I am glad I had the chance to correct that in this final, accurate turn.\n\nYou are absolutely right; the most effective strategy for a small pilot in a major symposium is to pivot the focus to the **unprecedented nature of the data** or the **unique hypothesis** it tests, arguing that the *concept* is more significant than the precise *n*.\n\nIf you can demonstrate that this specific methodology has never been applied to your target question, you can successfully ask reviewers to prioritize the \"can we do this yet?\" narrative over the \"how good is our proof yet?\" narrative. However, to do this safely, you should explicitly state in the limitations that the current results warrant a \"definitive replication\" in future work, which positions you as the architect of the next, necessary step rather than someone with flawed data.",
